:"Character encoding declared","pass":true,"detail":""},{"id":"lang","label":"Document language (lang) set","pass":true,"detail":"en"},{"id":"viewport","label":"Responsive viewport (zoom not disabled)","pass":true,"detail":"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"},{"id":"open-graph","label":"Open Graph tags (5/6)","pass":true,"detail":""},{"id":"twitter-card","label":"Twitter card metadata","pass":true,"detail":""},{"id":"open-social","label":"Open social presence (Mastodon/Bluesky) linked","pass":false,"detail":""}],"passed":8,"total":11,"social":[],"og":{"title":"Power Management Integration Center (PMIC)","description":"The Power Management Integration Center (PMIC) is developing next-generation technologies for integrated power electronics.Power electronics technologies are increasingly important for a wide range of applications, from handheld consumer electronics to renewable energy and electric vehicles. Almost anywhere electrical energy is used, power electronics - which convert electrical power from one form to another - offer improved efficiency and control. From advanced electrical and electronic systems to a single chip, nearly all power systems require more control, better management, and enhanced efficiency. Therefore, the need for improved power electronics is growing.PMIC is developing component, circuit, and system technologies to enable higher performance and new applications of power electronics, aiming for innovations to process and control energy efficiently and cost effectively. Advances in power converter topologies, integrated circuits, and passive components are integrated at the level of single chips, packages, and larger systems to achieve higher efficiency, smaller size, and reduced cost. Considerations are made for system reliability, robustness, and performance in a diverse range of industry sectors and applications including consumer, industrial, and automotive electronics.Research and development efforts span multiple levels in power electronics to support a variety of industry-sponsored projects and train the next generation of engineers in critical areas. In particular, PMIC explores new circuit architectures and design techniques that leverage high-level integration to expand the boundaries of efficiency and power density.","url":"/centers/integrated-power-management-circuits-and-systems---power-one-ic/","image":"/assets/images/twittercard.png","siteName":null,"type":"article"},"resilience":{"checks":[{"id":"pwa-https","label":"HTTPS (required for service workers)","status":"pass","evidence":"","exampleUrl":"https://iucrc.nsf.gov/centers/integrated-power-management-circuits-and-systems---power-one-ic/","why":"HTTPS is required for service workers and most install prompts."},{"id":"pwa-manifest","label":"Web app manifest declared","status":"fail","evidence":"","exampleUrl":"https://iucrc.nsf.gov/centers/integrated-power-management-circuits-and-systems---power-one-ic/","why":"A web app manifest is required for installability and controls how the app appears when launched."},{"id":"pwa-service-worker","label":"Service worker registered","status":"fail","evidence":"","exampleUrl":"https://iucrc.nsf.gov/centers/integrated-power-management-circuits-and-systems---power-one-ic/","why":"A service worker is what makes offline access, caching, and background sync possible."},{"id":"pwa-theme-color","label":"Theme color declared","status":"fail","evidence":"","exampleUrl":"https://iucrc.nsf.gov/centers/integrated-power-management-circuits-and-systems---power-one-ic/","why":"Theme color controls the browser chrome/status-bar color when the site is installed or bookmarked."},{"id":"pwa-apple-touch-icon","label":"Apple touch icon (iOS/bookmark icon)","status":"fail","evidence":"","exampleUrl":"https://iucrc.nsf.gov/centers/integrated-power-management-circuits-and-systems---power-one-ic/","why":"iOS uses this icon for home-screen bookmarks and \"Add to Home Screen\" — without it the icon falls back to a screen